#const AX 33

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT
    land_percent 15
    base_size 6
    land_position 25 25
  }

  AX
  {
    terrain_type SNOW
    land_percent 15
    base_size 6
    land_position 75 75
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_63 - does an unrecognised command's block merge into the command before it?
Feeds: docs/known-issues.md BUG-005 piece 2, parser-design Sec.2.1,
preview-design Sec.6.1
Run 61 and 62 first. This is the arm that makes their answer decisive.

WHAT 61 AND 62 RETURNED, AT NORMAL, SAME SETTINGS

  61  create_land DIRT, AL(32) SNOW, AX(33) DESERT  ->  DIRT 6063, DESERT 6233
  62  create_land DIRT, AX(33) SNOW, AL(32) DESERT  ->  SNOW 6043, DESERT 6191

Both runs produced exactly TWO lands of about 15 percent, and in each the missing
terrain is the one a single model names in advance.

  32 IS create_land. 33 is NOT a command. An unrecognised word's block MERGES
  into the command before it, and the later value of a repeated attribute wins.

61 under that model: create_land makes the DIRT land; AL is a real create_land
and makes the SNOW land; AX is not a command, so its block folds into the SNOW
land and rewrites the terrain to DESERT. DIRT plus DESERT, which is what came
back. 62: create_land starts the DIRT land, AX folds into it and rewrites it to
SNOW, AL makes the DESERT land. SNOW plus DESERT, which is also what came back.

The model that 61 alone suggested - that create_land is 33 and 32 is inert - is
refuted by 62, where 32 made a land.

WHY THIS RUN EXISTS
Two histograms fitting one model is agreement, not a measurement. The rival story
is ordinary engine behaviour already recorded in this project: a land whose
origin is buried by a later stamp before it grows is lost outright, which also
produces "one of my three lands is missing" and varies with the seed. It has to
be ruled out by constructing the case where the two disagree rather than by
preferring the tidier explanation.

METHOD
Two land commands only. A real create_land carrying DIRT at land_position 25 25,
then AX aliased to 33 carrying SNOW at land_position 75 75. Nothing else changes.

The positions are the whole reason this run can answer, and they were added
2026-08-13 after the first draft could not. Pinning both origins at opposite
corners makes a buried origin IMPOSSIBLE - the rival model needs one stamp to
land on the other's origin, and these are half a map apart. It also buys a
second, independent read-off: land_position is a repeated attribute, so under
the merge the surviving land sits where AX asked, not where create_land did.
Terrain and position are then two readings of one model, and they must agree.

READ-OFF

  SNOW about 6000, NO DIRT, and the land sits BOTTOM-RIGHT
      The merge is real. One land was created and both its terrain and its
      position were rewritten by a block belonging to a word the engine does not
      know. Later value wins on a repeated attribute, as 61 and 62 modelled.
  SNOW about 6000, NO DIRT, but the land sits TOP-LEFT
      A PARTIAL merge - the block is read, but not every attribute overrides.
      Interesting in its own right and it needs its own run per attribute; do not
      fold it into the simple model.
  DIRT about 6000 top-left AND SNOW about 6000 bottom-right
      No merge. 33 is a land command in its own right, and 61 and 62 were each
      losing a land some other way.
  DIRT about 6000 top-left only, no SNOW
      No merge and 33 is not a command - the block is simply discarded.
  Neither terrain
      Something ate the first command. Rerun before reading anything.

WHY THE ANSWER MATTERS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT REACH
Corrected 2026-08-13. This run was originally justified by the 457 misspelled
set_loose grouping lines in DE's Battle Royale maps. It does not reach them:
those sit INSIDE a create_object block with no braces of their own, so they are
misspelled attributes already inside the command they would supposedly merge
into, and the merge model only acts on a word that OPENS a block. Measured after
BUG-005 piece 2 landed, the corpus contains ZERO unknown commands carrying a
block, and all 292 install files contain only L in Petra plus this batch's AL/AX.

What it does reach is the population an expert corpus cannot show: a beginner
writing create_lands { by mistake in this very editor. If the merge holds, the
warning on a block-opening unknown command should say the block is being folded
into the command above, which is a far more useful thing to tell an author than
that the name is unrecognised. Nothing is blocked on this run.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count, ONE RUN. Same settings as 61 and 62
or the comparison is void. Read with --bbox or --patches, not the histogram
alone, since position is half the answer.
*/
